-\paragraph{The nine adjudications.} Nine judgments were changed by hand from incorrect to correct. All nine concern the controller's outputs, none changed in the other direction, and seven fall in the single-session-preference type (Figure~\ref{fig:lme}). Eight of the nine changed a verdict of the model judge and one changed a verdict from a deterministic guard. The nine identifiers are a hard-coded list in three scripts, each with a short written reason, and the original judge output is kept in backup files. The nine cases were adjudicated by an independent human reviewer, but no file records why these nine were chosen or how many of the 52 failures of the controller were reviewed. The same procedure was not applied to the baselines; their three overrides came from a separate pass by an independent human reviewer, and the available files do not record the selection criteria or review coverage for that pass. The frozen analysis plan asks that results be reported on the raw judge score first and that any adjudication be reported as a separate sensitivity analysis; we therefore treat 128 of 180 as the primary count and 137 of 180 as a one-sided post-hoc sensitivity count, which should not be read as an independently adjudicated accuracy. Seven of the nine reviewed cases were in the preference type; how the cases were selected and how many other failures were examined are not documented, so we draw no conclusion about where the model judge and a human reader disagree.
